tasha: Quick thread for PioneerMed POC. They need a single TLS WebSocket that multiplexes sessions, streaming token output, built-in backpressure, in-stream function/tool calls (they call them tool_rpc), and strict Protobuf/Avro structured output validated against a schema registry. Also EU residency, mTLS option, and short-lived certs. They want a tiny Rust snippet + concise runbook and a legal note before tomorrow.

kevin: We can support Rust via the websocket + gRPC-style tool_rpc over the stream. For Protobuf outputs we recommend shipping a content-type header and base64-encoded bytes for binary frames, or use text frames with base64 payload + schema_id. We don't currently host a managed schema registry, but we accept schema_id references and can wire to customer's registry URL for validation on Dedicated/Private.

omar: Important ops notes: 1) For exactly-ordered tool calls use Dedicated with sticky routing. 2) Backpressure: clients must honor server WINDOW frames; we also support an application-level token bucket for QPS shaping. 3) mTLS/short-lived certs: we can issue ephemeral client certs via our CSR signing endpoint (rotate default 30m).

frank: Minimal Rust example (copy-pasteable) — shows opening a ws, sending open with schema_id, handling tool_rpc event and replying. Keep it small: 

```rust
// tokio + tungstenite sketch
use tokio_tungstenite::connect_async;
use futures_util::{SinkExt, StreamExt};

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {
  let (ws_stream, _) = connect_async(std::env::var("POC_WS_URL")?).await?;
  let (mut write, mut read) = ws_stream.split();
  // open logical session with schema_id
  let open = r#"{\"type\":\"open\",\"session_id\":\"s1\",\"schema_id\":\"proto:v1/patient\"}"#;
  write.send(tungstenite::Message::Text(open.to_string())).await?;

  while let Some(msg) = read.next().await {
    let msg = msg?;
    if let tungstenite::Message::Text(txt) = msg {
      if txt.contains("tool_rpc") {
        // parse call_id, args; call local adapter; respond with base64 protobuf
        let resp = r#"{\"type\":\"tool_response\",\"call_id\":\"c1\",\"payload_b64\":\"...\"}"#;
        write.send(tungstenite::Message::Text(resp.to_string())).await?;
      }
    }
  }
  Ok(())
}
```

kevin: Handshake curl for cert CSR flow (example):

curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/handshake \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $RW_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"session_id":"s1","schema_id":"proto:v1/patient","want_cert":"ephemeral"}'

// returns {"ws_url":"wss://...","cert_csr_url":"https://.../sign","expires_in":1800} -> POST CSR to sign endpoint -> get client cert -> open mTLS ws.

omar: Runbook (concise):
1) Exchange IdP metadata (if SSO) and customer's schema registry URL + any auth for registry.
2) Provision EU Dedicated test pool and enable ephemeral certs (2-3 days). Private on-prem install ~2-3 weeks.
3) POST /handshake with schema_id and want_cert flag -> get ws_url and cert CSR flow.
4) Obtain ephemeral cert, open mTLS websocket, send open with session_id + schema_id.
5) Multiplex sessions by session_id; obey server WINDOW/backpressure frames.
6) On tool_rpc events call adapter endpoint and respond with tool_response containing payload_b64 (protobuf) or json depending on agreed encoding.
7) After demo rotate/revoke ephemeral certs via /revoke-ephemeral.

frank: Legal note draft: "Runtime may keep transient inference state and KV caches in-memory only; by default Dedicated and Private deployments do not persist raw prompt/response tokens to disk unless customer enables logging. Where binary protocol frames (e.g., protobuf) are used they are treated like other request/response payloads and are not retained persistently unless logging is enabled. Customers can require customer-managed key material for at-rest artifacts and may use mTLS/short-lived certs for in-flight protections. Audit trails for provisioning and admin actions are available per contract."

kevin: Ballpark for 500 QPS sustained for 1 week (EU Dedicated test) — rough: $30k-$55k depending on avg tokens and binary sizes; cert signing and extra integration support can add to the short engagement. Formal quote from sales ops will follow.

